            <ArticleTitle>Treatment planning with intensity modulated particle therapy for multiple targets in stage IV non-small cell lung cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1088/1361-6560/aa9c62</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Intensity modulated particle therapy (IMPT) can produce highly conformal plans, but is limited in advanced lung cancer patients with multiple lesions due to motion and planning complexity. A 4D IMPT optimization including all motion states was expanded to include multiple targets, where each target (isocenter) is designated to specific field(s). Furthermore, to achieve stereotactic treatment planning objectives, target and OAR weights plus objective doses were automatically iteratively adapted. Finally, 4D doses were calculated for different motion scenarios. The results from our algorithm were compared to clinical stereotactic body radiation treatment (SBRT) plans. The study included 8 patients with 24 lesions in total. Intended dose regimen for SBRT was 24 Gy in 1 fraction, but lower fractionated doses had to be delivered in 3 cases due to OAR constraints or failed plan quality assurance (QA). The resulting IMPT treatment plans had no significant difference in target coverage compared to SBRT treatment plans. Average max point dose and dose to specific volume in OARs were on average 65% and 22% smaller with IMPT. IMPT could also deliver 24 Gy in 1 fraction in a patient where SBRT was limited due to the OAR vicinity. The developed algorithm shows the potential of IMPT in treatment of multiple moving targets in a complex geometry.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2017 Institute of Physics and Engineering in Medicine.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
